from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "3. Death, Destruction and Perishing in Their Own Contexts"),
("p", "Claim: the words most often cited for the end of the lost are used in Scripture of things that cease and of things that remain. The word alone does not decide which is meant. This chapter concedes what is true on the other side, shows the second use, and states what follows and what does not."),
("sub", "3.1 Where the words describe an ending"),
("p", "The words of destruction and death sometimes describe an end, and the volume says so first. Of the flood the record says, “All in whose nostrils was the breath of life, of all that was in the dry land, died” (Genesis 7:22), and in the next verse, “every living substance was destroyed which was upon the face of the ground” (Genesis 7:23). Destroyed and died stand side by side, and the destroying is the dying. When Nebuchadnezzar was angry with the wise men, he “commanded to destroy all the wise men of Babylon” (Daniel 2:12), and the destroying is killing. When the Pharisees “held a council against him, how they might destroy him” (Matthew 12:14), the word is again the word for taking life. Of the sons of Korah’s company the record is that “they perished from among the congregation” (Numbers 16:33) after the earth closed upon them."),
("p", "These are plain, and an annihilationist is right to cite them. Where a verse tells of a person put to death, destroy and perish describe the death. The volume does not deny it. The question is whether that is the only use of the words, and whether a verse in which they stand beside a judgment after death must be read as these are."),
("tbl", "Where destruction and perishing describe death.", [
    ("Genesis 7:22, 23", T("Gen 7:22-23"), "Died and destroyed describe the same event, the end of life on the ground."),
    ("Daniel 2:12", T("Dan 2:12"), "The command to destroy is a command to kill."),
    ("Matthew 12:14", T("Matt 12:14"), "To destroy him is to put him to death."),
    ("Numbers 16:33", T("Num 16:33"), "They perished from among the congregation, as the earth closed upon them."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "3.2 Where the person is lost and remains"),
("p", "The same family of words is used in the Gospels of persons who are not dead and who are not destroyed in the sense just seen. The Lord sends the Twelve “to the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matthew 10:6). The sheep is Israel, and Israel is alive. In the parable the shepherd, “having an hundred sheep, if he lose one of them, doth not leave the ninety and nine in the wilderness, and go after that which is lost, until he find it” (Luke 15:4). The sheep that is lost is a sheep in the wilderness, and when it is found the shepherd says, “I have found my sheep which was lost” (Luke 15:6). The psalmist says of himself, “I have gone astray like a lost sheep; seek thy servant” (Psalm 119:176), and he is the one speaking."),
("p", "The prodigal is spoken of in the same way. The father says, “this my son was dead, and is alive again; he was lost, and is found” (Luke 15:24), and the elder brother is told, “this thy brother was dead, and is alive again; and was lost, and is found” (Luke 15:32). The son was dead and lost while he lived, and the father’s speech is not about a corpse. The Lord gives the purpose of His coming in the same terms: “the Son of man is come to save that which was lost” (Matthew 18:11), and “to seek and to save that which was lost” (Luke 19:10). That which is lost is sought and found. It has not ceased to be."),
("p", "The point is limited. These verses are about persons in this life, and the parables of Luke 15 are Parable Study territory. They show that the word for lost and perishing is used of a living person in a condition of ruin or distance, and they do not say what becomes of the persons beyond the judgment. The volume draws only the lexical conclusion: the word does not mean cease to be in every verse in which it stands."),
("sub", "3.3 Where the thing destroyed remains"),
("p", "The same is true of things. “Neither do men put new wine into old bottles: else the bottles break, and the wine runneth out, and the bottles perish: but they put new wine into new bottles, and both are preserved” (Matthew 9:17). The bottles perish and the verb is set against being preserved. The skins are burst and useless, and they are there on the ground. In Mark 14 the disciples ask of the ointment, “Why was this waste of the ointment made?” (Mark 14:4), and Matthew puts it, “To what purpose is this waste?” (Matthew 26:8). The word is the noun of the same family, and the ointment was not annihilated. It was poured out."),
("p", "Peter says that the world that then was “being overflowed with water, perished” (2 Peter 3:6). In the verse before he has said that the earth was “standing out of the water and in the water” (2 Peter 3:5), and in the verse after, the heavens and the earth “which are now” are reserved “unto fire against the day of judgment and perdition of ungodly men” (2 Peter 3:7). The ordered world of that time perished, and the earth remained under the water. The letter names its readers, and the volume reads it so; it is cited here for how the word is used, and the passage is argued by no one in this chapter."),
("p", "Nations are spoken of in the same way. Pharaoh’s servants say to him, “knowest thou not yet that Egypt is destroyed?” (Exodus 10:7), and Egypt was standing. Of Moab the LORD says, “Moab shall be destroyed from being a people” (Jeremiah 48:42), and at the end of the chapter, “Yet will I bring again the captivity of Moab in the latter days” (Jeremiah 48:47). Moab is destroyed as a people and is afterwards brought back from captivity. Hosea says to Israel, “My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge” (Hosea 4:6), and they are still addressed as his people. Paul’s word for the discipline of the offender at Corinth is “the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus” (1 Corinthians 5:5). The destruction is of the flesh, and the spirit is saved."),
("tbl", "Where what is destroyed or lost remains.", [
    ("Matthew 10:6", T("Matt 10:6"), "The lost sheep is Israel, who is alive and is sent to."),
    ("Luke 15:24", T("Luke 15:24"), "Dead and lost are said of a son who is alive and speaking."),
    ("Matthew 9:17", T("Matt 9:17"), "The bottles perish and are set against being preserved; they are ruined, not gone."),
    ("Exodus 10:7", T("Exod 10:7"), "Egypt is destroyed and is still there."),
    ("Jeremiah 48:42", T("Jer 48:42"), "Moab is destroyed from being a people, and later restored from captivity."),
    ("1 Corinthians 5:5", T("1Cor 5:5"), "Destruction of the flesh, with the spirit saved."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "3.4 Death as a condition"),
("p", "The word for death is used in two ways too. In the garden the word is, “in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die” (Genesis 2:17), and the prophet writes, “the soul that sinneth, it shall die” (Ezekiel 18:4). An annihilationist reads these as the end of life, and the volume grants that they can be read so; Ezekiel 18:4 is among the strongest verses on that side, and it is answered in chapter 8. But the writers of the New Testament speak of death as a state of the living: “you hath he quickened, who were dead in trespasses and sins” (Ephesians 2:1), and the father’s “was dead, and is alive again” of Luke 15:24 stands beside it. The letter to Ephesus is cited here for the use of the word, and the setting of its readers is not in question."),
("p", "So death, like destruction, is not one thing in the text. It may be the end of life, and it may be the condition of one who is alive and cut off. A verse that says the wages of sin is death does not say which, and the volume does not read it as if it did."),
("sub", "3.5 What follows, and what does not"),
("p", "Three statements follow, and the volume marks them. It is Stated that destroy, perish, lost and death are used in Scripture of things that cease and of things that remain. It is Shown by the context that the second use is not rare: it occurs in the Gospels, the law, the prophets and the letters, of persons, nations and things. It is Inferred that a destruction verse set beside a judgment after death cannot be read from the word alone, and that the passage and its neighbours have to decide."),
("p", "Three statements do not follow. It does not follow that the lost do not cease to exist; the chapter has only cleared the ground. It does not follow that the destruction verses are all about ruin; where a verse says a person was put to death, it says so, and the volume does not blur that. And it does not follow that the words of the other side are weak. Where an annihilationist says that destroy often means kill, he is right, and the cases above are the evidence."),
("p", "Two neighbours are cited and not repeated. The oracles against the nations, among them Moab, are read in Volume 3 of The Prophets Series, The Nations Judged, with their own question. The Lord’s mission to the lost sheep of the house of Israel is read in Volume 2 of the Gospel Commentary Series, The Lord’s Ministry to Israel. This chapter takes from them only the use of the words."),
("ctbl", "The two uses of the words, side by side.", [
    ("Word", "Where it describes an end", "Where the thing remains"),
    ("Destroy", "Genesis 7:23; Daniel 2:12; Matthew 12:14", "Exodus 10:7; Jeremiah 48:42; 1 Corinthians 5:5"),
    ("Perish", "Numbers 16:33", "Matthew 9:17; 2 Peter 3:6"),
    ("Lost", "Not used of the end of a living person in these passages", "Matthew 10:6; Luke 15:4, 6, 24, 32; Luke 19:10"),
    ("Death", "Genesis 7:22; Genesis 2:17 on one reading", "Luke 15:24; Ephesians 2:1"),
  ], [0.15, 0.42, 0.43]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Destroy and perish describe death in Genesis 7, Daniel 2, Matthew 12 and Numbers 16, and the volume grants it.",
    "The same words are used of persons who are lost and remain, of things ruined and still there, and of nations destroyed as a people and later restored.",
    "Death is used of the end of life and of the condition of one who is alive and cut off.",
    "The word alone does not decide; the passage and its neighbours have to, which is the work of the chapters that follow.",
  ]),
]
